.TH head "" "Command"
.SH NAME
head \- print the beginning (or all but the end) of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBhead [{+\-}\fIn\fB[lbc]] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
head -- print the beginning (or all but the end) of a file
Usage:	head [{+-}n[lbc]] [file]
A leading '+' measures 'n' from the beginning of the file; a
leading '-' measures 'n' from the end.  The following letter
selects lines (default), blocks ('b') or characters ('c').
If the whole specifier is omitted, '+4l' is assumed.
If 'file' is omitted, the standard input is used.
.HE
.B head
copies part of
.IR file ,
or of the standard input if
.I file
is not given, to the standard output.
.PP
The optional specifier gives the leading
.B +
or
.BR \- ,
a count
.IR n ,
and an optional trailing letter
.BR l ,
.BR b ,
or
.BR c .
A
.B +
measures
.I n
lines, blocks, or characters from the beginning of the file, and
.B head
copies input up to and including that point.
A
.B \-
measures
.I n
from the end of the file, and
.B head
copies all but the last
.I n
lines, blocks, or characters.
The trailing letter selects the unit:
.BR l
for lines (the default if the letter is omitted),
.B b
for 512-byte blocks, or
.B c
for characters.
If the specifier is omitted entirely,
.B "+4l"
is assumed, copying the first four lines.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR dd ,
.BR egrep ,
.B tail
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B head
prints
.B "Can't open %s"
if
.I file
cannot be opened, and
.B "Window too large"
if a
.B \-
count in blocks or lines is too large to buffer, then exits with a
nonzero status.
